1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,327
A colleague deployed the nightly report service.

2
00:00:08,327 --> 00:00:11,119
Hours later it still has not run once.

3
00:00:11,667 --> 00:00:13,136
What is broken?

4
00:00:13,136 --> 00:00:15,781
The Pod is never scheduled.

5
00:00:16,333 --> 00:00:18,341
The Pod requests cpu:

6
00:00:18,341 --> 00:00:23,888
"64", 64 whole CPUs (the author meant 64m, 64 millicores).

7
00:00:23,888 --> 00:00:30,773
No node can offer that, so the scheduler leaves the Pod Pending forever.

8
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:35,707
Request what the application really needs.

9
00:00:35,707 --> 00:00:40,468
Fix the manifest and apply it; the Deployment replaces the Pod:

10
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:44,443
Pending + NODE <none> = a scheduling problem:

11
00:00:44,443 --> 00:00:47,323
read the FailedScheduling event first.

12
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:52,271
The new queue worker was deployed this morning.

13
00:00:52,271 --> 00:00:55,611
Its Pod shows a restart count that keeps growing.

14
00:00:56,167 --> 00:00:57,893
What should happen?

15
00:00:57,893 --> 00:00:59,891
A long-running worker.

16
00:01:00,433 --> 00:01:06,734
The worker needs QUEUE_URL and refuses to start without it; the Deployment does not set it.

17
00:01:06,734 --> 00:01:13,173
Kubernetes restarts the container exactly as configured, so it fails the same way every time.

18
00:01:14,933 --> 00:01:21,048
Give the container its configuration (in real life from a ConfigMap, lesson 11):

19
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:27,150
CrashLoopBackOff is not the error; it is Kubernetes waiting between restarts.

20
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:35,125
A web server was deployed "with the usual nginx image", but it never starts.

21
00:01:35,125 --> 00:01:35,945
Set it up:

22
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:38,572
Which object is involved?

23
00:01:38,572 --> 00:01:43,712
The kubelet tried to download the image named in the Pod spec,

24
00:01:43,712 --> 00:01:50,095
from the registry (docker.io/library/nginx), and the registry said not found.

25
00:01:50,633 --> 00:01:52,142
A typo in the tag:

26
00:01:52,142 --> 00:01:54,153
alpne instead of alpine.

27
00:01:54,153 --> 00:01:59,768
No such image exists, so the kubelet can never start the container.

28
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:05,789
ErrImagePull / ImagePullBackOff:

29
00:02:05,789 --> 00:02:13,367
read the Failed to pull image event, it quotes the exact name and the registry's answer.

30
00:02:13,367 --> 00:02:21,548
not found = wrong name or tag; unauthorized = credentials; 429 = rate limit; timeout = network.

31
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:28,424
The team wants a "toolbox" Pod with BusyBox to run debugging commands in.

32
00:02:28,424 --> 00:02:32,565
It keeps restarting, although nothing is wrong in its logs.

33
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:35,515
Exit code 0, reason Completed:

34
00:02:35,515 --> 00:02:39,862
the container did not crash, it finished successfully.

35
00:02:39,862 --> 00:02:44,210
A container lives exactly as long as its main process.

36
00:02:44,767 --> 00:02:47,879
The container has no long-running process.

37
00:02:47,879 --> 00:02:55,436
Deployments are for programs that run until stopped; a command that ends belongs in a Job (lesson 17).

38
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:04,365
Give the toolbox something that keeps running (here sleep), so the team can kubectl exec into it:

39
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:12,450
A container runs as long as its main process; a Deployment restarts anything that ends, even successfully.

40
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:20,212
The backend Pods run fine, but every request to the backend Service fails.

41
00:03:20,212 --> 00:03:21,025
Set it up:

42
00:03:21,567 --> 00:03:25,274
Which object decides where a Service sends traffic?

43
00:03:25,274 --> 00:03:26,220
Its selector:

44
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:30,436
every ready Pod whose labels match it becomes an endpoint.

45
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,613
A typo in the Service selector.

46
00:03:33,613 --> 00:03:39,935
Labels match exactly; one character is enough to match nothing (lesson 07).

47
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,322
"Service does not work" → first kubectl describe service:

48
00:03:47,322 --> 00:03:48,696
an empty Endpoints:

49
00:03:48,696 --> 00:03:53,468
points at the selector or at Pods that are not ready (problem 06).

50
00:03:53,468 --> 00:03:59,615
kubectl get pods -l SELECTOR with the Service's exact selector shows what it matches.

51
00:04:01,367 --> 00:04:08,075
After someone "added health checks", the backend Pods run but the Service sends them nothing.

52
00:04:08,075 --> 00:04:08,796
Set it up:

53
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:10,210
statuscode:

54
00:04:10,210 --> 00:04:10,529
404:

55
00:04:10,529 --> 00:04:17,547
the kubelet reached the application (so the port is right), but the path does not exist.

56
00:04:17,547 --> 00:04:23,368
Which path does the probe ask, and which paths does the application have?

57
00:04:23,933 --> 00:04:29,637
The readiness probe checks /ready, a path the application does not have.

58
00:04:29,637 --> 00:04:35,658
Every check gets 404, so the Pods never become ready and receive no traffic.

59
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:42,629
Running + 0/1 READY = a readiness problem:

60
00:04:42,629 --> 00:04:45,204
the Unhealthy event says why.

61
00:04:46,967 --> 00:04:50,933
The backend's configuration moved into a ConfigMap.

62
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:53,421
Since then its Pod never starts.

63
00:04:53,967 --> 00:04:59,216
The ConfigMap exists, but the key the Deployment asks for does not.

64
00:04:59,216 --> 00:05:01,096
Which keys does it have?

65
00:05:01,633 --> 00:05:05,967
The Deployment refers to a ConfigMap key that does not exist.

66
00:05:05,967 --> 00:05:13,428
A missing key (or ConfigMap) blocks the container from starting, unless the reference is marked optional:

67
00:05:13,428 --> 00:05:13,783
true.

68
00:05:15,533 --> 00:05:18,063
Point the reference at the real key:

69
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:30,843
CreateContainerConfigError = a ConfigMap, Secret or key the Pod needs is missing:

70
00:05:30,843 --> 00:05:34,924
the event names it exactly.

71
00:05:36,667 --> 00:05:42,417
The database password was put into a Secret, and the backend was told to read it.

72
00:05:42,417 --> 00:05:44,191
The backend never starts.

73
00:05:44,733 --> 00:05:47,579
Which Secrets exist in the namespace?

74
00:05:47,579 --> 00:05:51,963
(Listing Secrets shows their names, never their values.).

75
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:57,253
The Deployment references a Secret name that does not exist in its namespace,

76
00:05:57,253 --> 00:06:00,709
so the kubelet cannot build the container's environment.

77
00:06:03,967 --> 00:06:12,322
secret "X" not found = a wrong name or the wrong namespace; couldn't find key = a wrong key (problem 07).

78
00:06:12,322 --> 00:06:20,121
kubectl get secrets -n NS lists names safely; never print a Secret's values while troubleshooting.

79
00:06:21,867 --> 00:06:26,153
A Pod that writes data to a volume never starts.

80
00:06:26,153 --> 00:06:27,046
Set it up:

81
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,714
Which StorageClasses does the cluster have?

82
00:06:31,267 --> 00:06:35,126
The PVC names a StorageClass that does not exist here,

83
00:06:35,126 --> 00:06:40,271
so no provisioner creates a volume; the Pod waits for the claim forever.

84
00:06:40,271 --> 00:06:42,487
(Other causes of a Pending PVC:

85
00:06:42,487 --> 00:06:49,276
no default StorageClass, or a class with WaitForFirstConsumer that waits until a Pod uses it.).

86
00:06:51,033 --> 00:06:59,008
A PVC's storageClassName cannot be changed, so recreate the claim without it (the default class is used):

87
00:06:59,567 --> 00:07:02,179
A Pod Pending because of a volume:

88
00:07:02,179 --> 00:07:04,714
look at the PVC, then its events.

89
00:07:04,714 --> 00:07:12,396
kubectl get storageclass shows what this cluster can provision; class names differ between clusters.

90
00:07:14,133 --> 00:07:18,971
The shop's website should answer on shop.local through the Ingress.

91
00:07:18,971 --> 00:07:24,243
The web Pod runs, the Service exists, but the site answers with an error.

92
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:31,503
The rule sends / to a Service called frontend, and Kubernetes says it does not exist.

93
00:07:31,503 --> 00:07:33,159
Which Services exist?

94
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:41,799
The Ingress points at a Service name (frontend) that does not exist in its namespace; the Service is called web.

95
00:07:45,033 --> 00:07:47,629
Ingress problems are a chain:

96
00:07:47,629 --> 00:07:52,998
controller → rule (host, path) → Service → endpoints → Pods.

97
00:07:54,733 --> 00:07:59,130
Version 3.0.0 of the backend was released this afternoon.

98
00:07:59,130 --> 00:08:03,603
The deploy step has been "waiting for rollout" ever since.

99
00:08:04,167 --> 00:08:10,223
The rolling update started one Pod with the new version, and that Pod cannot pull its image.

100
00:08:10,223 --> 00:08:15,159
The Deployment waits for it to become ready before replacing more old Pods,

101
00:08:15,159 --> 00:08:17,463
so the three old Pods keep serving:

102
00:08:17,463 --> 00:08:20,491
no outage, just a rollout that never finishes.

103
00:08:21,033 --> 00:08:24,668
The release references an image that was never built.

104
00:08:24,668 --> 00:08:31,663
The rolling update protects the users (old Pods stay), but the rollout stays stuck until someone acts.

105
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:39,439
Go back to the last working version (lesson 09), then deal with the release:

106
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:46,934
A stuck rollout = a new Pod that never becomes ready; look at the new Pods, not the old ones.

107
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:52,655
The nightly import Pod stops in the middle of its work.

108
00:08:52,655 --> 00:08:54,885
Its logs end without any error.

109
00:08:55,433 --> 00:08:57,598
How did the container end?

110
00:08:58,133 --> 00:09:02,795
The import needs about 100 MiB but is limited to 48 MiB.

111
00:09:02,795 --> 00:09:06,291
A memory limit is a hard wall (lesson 15):

112
00:09:06,291 --> 00:09:10,953
crossing it kills the process, it does not slow it down.

113
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:19,720
Give the job the memory it needs (a Pod's resources cannot be changed in place, so recreate it):

114
00:09:20,267 --> 00:09:29,291
OOMKilled / exit code 137 / logs that just stop = the memory limit, not a bug in the logs.

115
00:09:31,033 --> 00:09:35,992
The reporting team's Pod, in its own namespace, must call the backend.

116
00:09:35,992 --> 00:09:45,413
"It works from the backend's namespace but not from ours." Set up the backend in trouble-13 and a client namespace trouble-13-client:

117
00:09:45,967 --> 00:09:54,821
A short Service name is completed with the caller's namespace (the search line of the Pod's /etc/resolv.conf):

118
00:09:55,367 --> 00:09:59,931
The client uses the short name backend from another namespace.

119
00:09:59,931 --> 00:10:05,011
Short names only work inside the Service's own namespace (lesson 10).

120
00:10:06,767 --> 00:10:12,362
Use the Service's name with its namespace (backend.trouble-13),

121
00:10:12,362 --> 00:10:17,336
or the full name (backend.trouble-13.svc.cluster.local):

122
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:25,805
bad address / Name or service not known = DNS; first prove DNS works with kubernetes.default,

123
00:10:25,805 --> 00:10:27,505
then check the name.

124
00:10:29,267 --> 00:10:31,663
Security locked the backend down:

125
00:10:31,663 --> 00:10:33,987
"only the frontend may call it".

126
00:10:33,987 --> 00:10:36,891
Now the frontend cannot reach it either.

127
00:10:37,433 --> 00:10:44,853
backend-deny-all blocks all incoming traffic; backend-allow-frontend should open it for the frontend.

128
00:10:44,853 --> 00:10:46,029
Policies add up:

129
00:10:46,029 --> 00:10:49,188
traffic is allowed if any policy allows it.

130
00:10:49,733 --> 00:10:57,185
The allow rule selects the frontend by a label it does not have, so only backend-deny-all takes effect:

131
00:10:57,185 --> 00:11:00,658
every caller, the frontend included, is dropped.

132
00:11:03,900 --> 00:11:08,240
A timeout to a Service with endpoints = traffic dropped:

133
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:10,099
suspect a NetworkPolicy.
